## Move dates back or forward

::: {#ce4686f6 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(df
 .with_columns(One_Month_Past=pl.col('Date').dt.offset_by('-1mo'))
 .with_columns(Five_Days_Future=pl.col('Date').dt.offset_by('5d'))
 )
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (3, 4)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Appointment</th><th>Date</th><th>One_Month_Past</th><th>Five_Days_Future</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>date</td><td>date</td><td>date</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Dentist&quot;</td><td>2026-06-02</td><td>2026-05-02</td><td>2026-06-07</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Doctor&quot;</td><td>2026-07-15</td><td>2026-06-15</td><td>2026-07-20</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Therapist&quot;</td><td>2026-09-05</td><td>2026-08-05</td><td>2026-09-10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


::: {.callout-note}
* To move dates closer to the present, use a negative value such as `-1mo`.
* To move dates farther into the future, use a positive value such as `5d`.
:::

